## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJvGHjdWMAIlqaJ?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560/Voxyz%5Fai/article/2061782441606451381/media/2061459087531847682) Dynamic Workflows: Claude Code Is Moving the Agent Plan from Chat into Executable Scripts...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Vox</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/Voxyz_ai">@Voxyz_ai</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560">http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560">http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJvGHjdWMAIlqaJ?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560/Voxyz%5Fai/article/2061782441606451381/media/2061459087531847682)<br/>Dynamic Workflows: Claude Code Is Moving the Agent Plan from Chat into Executable Scripts<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1998303259279138816/5bqsxKJS_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560/Voxyz%5Fai)<br/>[Vox](http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560/Voxyz%5Fai)<br/>[@Voxyz\_ai](http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560/Voxyz%5Fai)<br/>·<br/>19h<br/>·<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow Voxyz\_ai<br/>2<br/>12<br/>100<br/>[13K](http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560/Voxyz%5Fai/status/2061782441606451381/analytics)<br/>Claude Code shipped Dynamic Workflows a few days ago. Most people's first reaction: "finally, hundreds of subagents at once."<br/>That's half right.<br/>To be clear, "hundreds" doesn't mean hundreds running at the same time. The official docs cap concurrent agents at 16, with a maximum of 1,000 agents per workflow run. The real new thing is that the plan gets written as an executable script.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJzz4nBXwAAGfae?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2061458737726914560/Voxyz%5Fai/article/2061782441606451381/media/2061790883301801984)<br/>Subagents solve who does the work. Dynamic Workflows change something more fundamental: where the plan lives.<br/>Before this, the plan lived in chat context. Claude would think one step, act one step, read results as they came. Subagents could handle side tasks, skills could teach it a method, but the thing actually deciding what happens next was still jammed into the conversation window.<br/>Dynamic Workflows pull the plan out of chat. Claude writes an orchestration script. Claude Code executes it directly. Loops, branches, intermediate state, review chains, failure retries, all run inside the script. The main conversation gets a clean summary at the end, not the entire messy middle.<br/>Before, running a big task with Claude Code was like standing on a construction site shouting orders one by one. Dig the foundation. Done. Pour the columns. Done. What's next?<br/>The more workers you had, the more your voice became the bottleneck. Because what was really stuck wasn't who does the work, but who remembers the whole blueprint.<br/>What Dynamic Workflows do is pull the blueprint out of chat. Blueprint = plan. Foreman = workflow script. Crews = subagents. You = the client.<br/>In practice it looks like this. You type one message in Claude Code:<br/>plaintext<br/>```plaintext<br/>Audit every API endpoint in this project.<br/>Check authentication, error handling, and documentation coverage.<br/>```<br/>Claude decides this task fits a workflow. It writes an orchestration script with several phases: scan all endpoints, check each one, aggregate issues, generate a report. You glance at the phase breakdown, confirm it looks right, and it starts running.<br/>You can watch progress as it goes: which phase is running, how many agents are active, whether any permission prompts came up. When it finishes, you get a structured audit report, not dozens of scattered findings floating in chat.<br/>Anthropic picked Bun as the proof case for this feature. Jarred Sumner used it to port Bun from Zig to Rust. Roughly 750,000 lines of Rust, 99.8% of existing tests passing, eleven days from first commit to merge. The official blog also notes this port was still outside production at the time. I treat it as an anatomy specimen.<br/>This article covers the 5 rules I use to evaluate agent workflows. Each comes with a module you can take and use directly.<br/>## <br/>1\. First question: where does the plan live<br/>When I look at an agent workflow, the first thing I check is where the plan lives:<br/>plaintext<br/>```plaintext<br/>Where plan lives          Good for                           Main problem<br/>Chat conte</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 107 · 🔁 12 · 💬 2 · 🔖 190</p><p><a href="https://x.com/Voxyz_ai/status/2061782441606451381">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
