      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Kpaxs</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/Kpaxs">@Kpaxs</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000">http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000">http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>Did someone say … cookies?<br/>X and its partners use cookies to provide you with a better, safer and faster service and to support our business. Some cookies are necessary to use our services, improve our services, and make sure they work properly. Show more about your choices.<br/>Accept all cookies<br/>Refuse non-essential cookies<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJtTJVYWYAIeJvh?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000/Kpaxs/article/2061435378024739198/media/2061332674275336194)<br/>The Agency Skill Nobody Talks About<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1289428895/eightbit-ce6d25c8-fbf2-468d-a8d4-43f7d5502b19_normal.png)](http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000/Kpaxs)<br/>[Kpaxs](http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000/Kpaxs)<br/>[@Kpaxs](http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000/Kpaxs)<br/>·<br/>12h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow Kpaxs<br/>2<br/>7<br/>61<br/>[3.4K](http://x.com/i/article/2061324501170688000/Kpaxs/status/2061435378024739198/analytics)<br/>You are far freer than you feel, and the gap between your real freedom and your felt freedom is occupied almost entirely by a person who isn't there.<br/>I.<br/>When you decide whether to do some hard, scary, agentic thing (quit the job, ship the weird project, say the true thing in the tense room), you imagine consequences. And the consequences you imagine almost always feature a particular character. He's a sort of composite figure: part judge, part audience, part disappointed parent, part the smartest person you know. He watches what you do and renders a verdict. When you picture the cold-email going unanswered, he's the one who knows you sent it and failed. When you picture the project flopping, he's the one shaking his head. When you imagine asking for the impossible thing and being refused, he's the one who saw you overreach.<br/>I want to make a strong claim: he does not exist. There is no such person. There is no unified observer tracking your attempts, comparing them against your station, and updating a permanent ledger of how foolish you've been. And yet the fear of his verdict is, I'm increasingly convinced, the single largest force suppressing agency in competent adults. Not lack of skill. Not lack of options. Not even lack of courage in the raw sense. It's deference to a spectator who isn't there.<br/>I call him the Phantom Referee, and once you learn to look for him, you find his fingerprints on almost every door you've decided not to open.<br/>II.<br/>The first thing to notice about the Phantom Referee is that he's composite and therefore impossible. When you imagine being judged for asking your boss for a raise, who exactly is judging? It's not your boss, your boss will mostly forget the conversation by lunch. It's not your coworkers, who have their own raises to worry about. It's not your past self or your future self. It's a stitched-together figure who has the omniscience of God (he sees every attempt), the harshness of your worst critic (he assumes the worst reading), and the memory of an immortal (he never forgets). No actual human being combines these properties. Real observers are partial, distracted, forgetful, and mostly thinking about themselves. The Phantom Referee is the only entity in your life paying full, permanent, hostile attention to you. And he's not real.<br/>This matters because the entire calculation that suppresses agency runs through him. The expected cost of the agentic act is almost never the actual social cost, which is usually trivial. It's the cost as scored by the Referee, who inflates it enormously, because his whole function is to inflate it. He converts "I might get a polite no" into "I will be permanently marked as the kind of person who didn't know their place." And nobody would pay the second cost to avoid a polite no... so we don't act. We're not avoiding the real consequence. We're avoiding h</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 76 · 🔁 8 · 💬 1 · 🔖 127</p><p><a href="https://x.com/Kpaxs/status/2061435378024739198">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
