      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Dimitar Angelov</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/dimitarangg">@dimitarangg</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882">http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882</a></p></blockquote><div><img src="https://d3v0px0pttie1i.cloudfront.net/uploads/user/logo/47960481/opengraph_db994ee4.png?source=opengraph" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://d3v0px0pttie1i.cloudfront.net/uploads/user/logo/47960481/twitter_db994ee4.png?source=opengraph" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJvKcGkXsAAfaHJ.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/></div><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882">http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJqRBwfX0AI5cpf?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882/dimitarangg/article/2061119279177638025/media/2061119238857805826)<br/>how we use borderline illegal methods to scrape B2B leads<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1986433387662356480/FsPWe7_p_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882/dimitarangg)<br/>[Dimitar Angelov](http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882/dimitarangg)<br/>[@dimitarangg](http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882/dimitarangg)<br/>·<br/>May 31<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow dimitarangg<br/>1<br/>4<br/>27<br/>[3.5K](http://x.com/i/article/2061117982340730882/dimitarangg/status/2061119279177638025/analytics)<br/>the best leads in B2B outreach come from places that most people either don't know about or are too afraid to scrape<br/>here's every method we use to find leads across databases that our competitors have never touched, producing lead lists that get 2-3x higher reply rates than anything you'll pull from Apollo<br/>## <br/>the apollo problem and why we abandoned it<br/>apollo has 250 million contacts and is the first tool every cold outreach person buys, which means every meaningful decision-maker has been pitched dozens of times by agencies pulling from the same database<br/>we stopped using Apollo as our primary source two years ago and haven't looked back, our reply rates doubled within the first month of switching to alternative sources<br/>## <br/>method 1: google maps scraping at scale<br/>Google Maps contains every local and regional business with verified operating data, the scraping uses either Apify's Google Maps scraper or custom Python scripts with the Google Places API<br/>the workflow: search for \[business type\] in \[city\], extract business name, address, phone, website, reviews, and rating, filter for established businesses (20+ reviews, 4+ stars, 10+ years), then enrich with decision-maker emails through web scraping and Hunter<br/>one client targeting insurance agencies scraped 3,200 businesses across 50 cities and got reply rates of 4.8% compared to 1.5% on Apollo lists, the leads had never been pitched by cold email agencies because nobody thinks to scrape Maps for B2B outreach<br/>## <br/>method 2: linkedin behavioral scraping<br/>beyond demographic filters on Sales Navigator, we scrape behavioral signals that indicate buying intent<br/>job changers in the last 90 days using Phantombuster to extract profiles, these people reply at 45% because they're rebuilding their tech stack, LinkedIn post engagement scraping to find people who commented on content related to your offer topic, hiring signal scraping from LinkedIn Jobs to identify companies actively investing in functions related to your service<br/>## <br/>method 3: twitter/X engagement and follower scraping<br/>scraping commenters and likers from niche tweets reveals people who self-identified as interested in your topic, reply rates on X-engagement-sourced lists run 6-8% compared to 2% from demographic-only sources<br/>follower graph scraping: extracting followers of 5-10 niche authority accounts gives you tens of thousands of self-qualified prospects who follow content about your service area<br/>giveaway participant scraping from niche giveaways (not mainstream ones that attract low-quality followers) produces concentrated pools of your exact ICP<br/>## <br/>method 4: crunchbase funding data scraping<br/>recently funded companies have fresh budget, growth mandates, and maximum openness to cold outreach<br/>we query Crunchbase for companies that received funding in the last 90 days in specific verticals, export founding team and executive profiles, and enrich with email addresses<br/>the personalization hook is built in: "saw the Series A announcement, congrats, curious how you're handling \[problem\] now that you're scaling"<br/>(we deploy these scraping methods as part of our full outbound sy</blockquote><p>🔗 <a href="https://calendly.com/synergyaisolutions/discovery-call">Calendly</a></p><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 29 · 🔁 4 · 💬 1 · 🔖 81</p><p><a href="https://x.com/dimitarangg/status/2061119279177638025">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
