## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJqMqc8aQAA0nyJ?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336/MichLieben/article/2061114475797946764/media/2061114440427388928) The 7 APIs That Turn Claude Code Into A Full Cold Email Engine...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Michel Lieben</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/MichLieben">@MichLieben</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336">http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336">http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJqMqc8aQAA0nyJ?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336/MichLieben/article/2061114475797946764/media/2061114440427388928)<br/>The 7 APIs That Turn Claude Code Into A Full Cold Email Engine<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2032185394473955329/sT3lY5XL_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336/MichLieben)<br/>[Michel Lieben](http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336/MichLieben)<br/>[@MichLieben](http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336/MichLieben)<br/>·<br/>May 31<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow MichLieben<br/>3<br/>12<br/>44<br/>[8.4K](http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336/MichLieben/status/2061114475797946764/analytics)<br/>Most cold email stacks look like a Rube Goldberg machine. You export a list from Apollo, paste it into Clay, run waterfall enrichment, sanity-check the data, push to Lemlist, then jump back to Smartlead to monitor analytics. Five tabs open at once, three CSV exports done by hand, two manual joins to align lead IDs. By the time the campaign is live, the person who built it can't remember which step had stale data.<br/>That model still runs most outbound teams. It works, but every tool is its own walled garden, and the operator spends more time moving CSVs around than thinking about who they're actually targeting.<br/>The new way is different. The whole stack collapses into one place. A terminal window running Claude Code, with API access to every data provider you'd normally bounce between in a browser. You type what you want in plain English. The agent loads the right skill, calls the right API, pipes the result into the next one, and hands you a launched campaign at the end.<br/>We've been running this exact setup at ColdIQ for client work after spending the last two years stitching together every flavor of GTM stack on the market across 400+ B2B companies. The article below is the full breakdown. Which 7 APIs to plug in and why each one. How to wire them as Claude Code skills. And the feedback loop that makes the system get smarter every campaign you launch.<br/>## <br/>Why the browser-first GTM stack is hitting a wall<br/>Clay solved a real problem when it launched. It gave operators a place to enrich, score, and reshape lead data without writing code. The trade-off was that everything still lives inside a spreadsheet UI. You're clicking, dragging columns, building tables. The cognitive load isn't gone. It just moved from CSVs to webhook chains.<br/>What changes with Claude Code is that the orchestration layer becomes a chat. You stop building the spreadsheet. You describe the outcome you want, and the agent figures out which APIs to call in which order. The APIs themselves haven't changed. The way you talk to them has.<br/>There's a second shift that matters more. Every step the agent takes leaves an artifact. The company list, the signal results, the contact list, the enrichment outputs, the campaign push. All of it lands in the working directory as files. Next campaign, the agent has full memory of what was targeted, what data we already have, and what worked.<br/>That's the piece no GUI tool gives you. The stack remembers.<br/># <br/>The 7-API stack at a glance<br/>Here's the pipeline we built for a recent walkthrough with Kenny on our YouTube channel. Each API does exactly one job. Together they take you from a vague targeting idea to a launched campaign with mobile numbers attached.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJZlp5aaMAAgj8T?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2059942499565838336/MichLieben/article/2061114475797946764/media/2059945650029211648)<br/>Pipeline flow<br/>The seven providers, in order of where they show up in the workflow:<br/>* AI Ark: company-level database with 70M+ companies. The starting point for list building. Strong API for filters like employee count, industry, funding stage, and location. Has</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 43 · 🔁 9 · 💬 3 · 🔖 124</p><p><a href="https://x.com/MichLieben/status/2061114475797946764">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
