## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJkbOluXkAAKnG6?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/bibryam/article/2060708326829170785/media/2060708241957425152) Coding Agents Need a Trust Layer...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Bilgin Ibryam</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/bibryam">@bibryam</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973">http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973">http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJkbOluXkAAKnG6?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/bibryam/article/2060708326829170785/media/2060708241957425152)<br/>Coding Agents Need a Trust Layer<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1779610675431591936/WMVtJ651_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/bibryam)<br/>[Bilgin Ibryam](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/bibryam)<br/>[@bibryam](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/bibryam)<br/>·<br/>2h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow bibryam<br/>2<br/>4<br/>31<br/>[2K](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/bibryam/status/2060708326829170785/analytics)<br/>Coding agents made writing code cheaper. Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, and similar tools can now produce useful diffs quickly. But they did not make code easier to trust.<br/>That is the next bottleneck.<br/>A green build only tells you the code compiled, formatted, and passed existing tests. It does not tell you whether the agent preserved business rules, wrote meaningful tests, respected architecture, or touched tools it should not have access to.<br/>The missing piece is an agent quality layer.<br/>It sits between the coding agent and the pull request. Its job is simple: catch mistakes before they become review work, and leave enough evidence behind so humans can understand what happened later.<br/>## <br/>1\. Feedback sensors<br/>A coding agent without compiler, lint, type, and test feedback turns the human reviewer into the first quality gate.<br/>That is backwards.<br/>Agents should see fast failures while they work, not after they open a PR.<br/>This is where Birgitta Böckeler’s work on maintainability sensors is useful. The lesson is simple: give agents feedback they can act on.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJkaQAAW0AYOMzQ?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/bibryam/article/2060708326829170785/media/2060707166680436742)<br/>Use compilers, type checks, focused tests, custom lint messages, mutation testing with tools like cargo-mutants, and fuzzing with tools like WuppieFuzz.<br/>The goal is not more process. The goal is fewer obvious mistakes reaching review.<br/>## <br/>2\. Semantic evals<br/>Some code compiles and still does the wrong thing.<br/>This happens with generated tests, policy logic, tool-use workflows, data transformations, and customer-facing behavior.<br/>Normal tests check outputs.<br/>Semantic evals check meaning.<br/>Use golden examples, DeepEval, LLM-as-judge with reviewed examples, hallucination checks, tool-use checks, and semantic review for behavior that is hard to verify with deterministic tests alone.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJkZ2y7WAAMroZQ?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/bibryam/article/2060708326829170785/media/2060706733673021443)<br/>This should not replace tests.<br/>It should catch the class of failures that tests often miss.<br/>## <br/>3\. Refactor boundaries<br/>Coding agents can rewrite code faster than humans can review it.<br/>That is useful in clean modules.<br/>It is dangerous in legacy hotspots.<br/>A 2,000-line class with years of business logic is exactly where an agent may confidently refactor code and accidentally delete behavior nobody documented.<br/>Teams need refactor boundaries.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJkZ87IWkAMC9S3?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2060705100503379973/bibryam/article/2060708326829170785/media/2060706838954283011)<br/>Use CodeScene to find hotspots. Use CODEOWNERS for risky paths. Use dependency-cruiser, ArchUnit, or Spring Modulith to enforce module boundaries.<br/>Give agents green zones for safe cleanup, yellow zones for scoped changes, and red zones where a human design step is required first.<br/>Speed is useful only when the blast radius is controlled.<br/>## <br/>4\. Provenance and agent surface inventory<br/>In an AI-assisted codebase, git blame is no longer eno</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 108 · 🔁 14 · 💬 4 · 🔖 207</p><p><a href="https://x.com/bibryam/status/2060708326829170785">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
