## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJimoi-akAAyKSQ?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512/%5Fheyrico/article/2060580554131771584/media/2060580045035573248) the 100x builder [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2058377902014812160/9QBWe5nI_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512/%5Fheyrico)...
- https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSUJhbcAEVkhx.png: https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSUJhbcAEVkhx.png
- https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSUZnb0AA9ejo.png: https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSUZnb0AA9ejo.png
- https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSUx7bcAAlqzz.png: https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSUx7bcAAlqzz.png
- https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSVG_bYAA0ep0.png: https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSVG_bYAA0ep0.png</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>rico</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/_heyrico">@_heyrico</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512">http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512</a></p></blockquote><div><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSUJhbcAEVkhx.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSUZnb0AA9ejo.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSUx7bcAAlqzz.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJuSVG_bYAA0ep0.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/></div><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512">http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJimoi-akAAyKSQ?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512/%5Fheyrico/article/2060580554131771584/media/2060580045035573248)<br/>the 100x builder<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2058377902014812160/9QBWe5nI_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512/%5Fheyrico)<br/>[rico](http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512/%5Fheyrico)<br/>[@\_heyrico](http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512/%5Fheyrico)<br/>·<br/>3h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow \_heyrico<br/>1<br/>3<br/>[246](http://x.com/i/article/2060561764451008512/%5Fheyrico/status/2060580554131771584/analytics)<br/>The 100x builder is not someone who types one hundred times faster.<br/>That version of the idea is a toy.<br/>The real shift is that one capable builder can now cover more of the product surface than before: research, design, code, copy, testing, documentation, launch assets, and iteration.<br/>AI did not remove the need for taste. It made taste easier to expose.<br/>OpenAI says more than 3 million developers use Codex weekly. It also says nearly all engineers inside OpenAI now use Codex and merge 70 percent more pull requests each week. GitHub's older Copilot study found a 55 percent speed gain on a controlled task. At the same time, METR found experienced open-source developers were 19 percent slower with early-2025 AI tools on a specific set of real tasks.<br/>So the honest story is not "AI makes everyone 100x."<br/>The honest story is sharper:<br/>AI multiplies the person who can direct work well.<br/>## <br/>## <br/>The Core Problem<br/>The old 10x engineer myth was about individual output.<br/>One rare person could supposedly write better code, make better decisions, and ship more than an average team. The idea was always a little messy because software work is not just typing. It is understanding the problem, choosing the right abstraction, working with constraints, reviewing tradeoffs, and keeping the system healthy.<br/>AI makes the myth more tempting and more dangerous.<br/>It is easy to watch an agent build a feature, generate a screen, write tests, or create a landing page and conclude that the builder has become superhuman.<br/>But that is not what is happening.<br/>The builder is not becoming faster at every task.<br/>The builder is changing the shape of the task.<br/>Instead of doing every step manually, the builder is becoming a director of work. They decide what needs to be done, prepare the context, choose the tool, inspect the result, correct the direction, and decide whether the output is good enough to ship.<br/>That is a different skill.<br/>It is closer to running a tiny product team than being a faster individual contributor.<br/>## <br/>## <br/>Explanation<br/>The 100x builder has range.<br/>Not shallow range where they can pretend to do everything. Useful range where they know enough about each layer to move the work forward.<br/>They can research the market before building.<br/>They can design a simple interface before writing code.<br/>They can write the first landing page before hiring a copywriter.<br/>They can use an agent to create a prototype.<br/>They can test the prototype in a browser.<br/>They can ask another model to critique the product logic.<br/>They can turn the result into a demo, post, or investor update.<br/>That does not mean every output is excellent.<br/>It means the waiting cost between disciplines is lower.<br/>A designer does not need to wait a week to see whether an interaction can work in code. A founder does not need to wait for a full team to test a product promise. A solo studio owner does not need five specialists for the first version of every asset.<br/>The bottleneck moves.<br/>Before AI, the bottleneck was often production capacity.<br/>Can we design it?<br/>Can we code it?<br/>Can we write it?<br/>Can we make the deck?<br/>Can we create the demo?<br/>After AI, the bottleneck is judgment.<br/>Is this the right thing to build?<br/>Is</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 12 · 🔁 2 · 💬 1 · 🔖 24</p><p><a href="https://x.com/_heyrico/status/2060580554131771584">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
