## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJfnQn4WsAM4o-D?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris/article/2060399898798018696/media/2060369627314565123) This Workspace Setup Will Change Your Life [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1655731071970054144/_0Onbx-...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Rian Doris</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/RianSweetDoris">@RianSweetDoris</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403">http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403">http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJfnQn4WsAM4o-D?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris/article/2060399898798018696/media/2060369627314565123)<br/>This Workspace Setup Will Change Your Life<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1655731071970054144/_0Onbx-a_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris)<br/>[Rian Doris](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris)<br/>[@RianSweetDoris](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris)<br/>·<br/>15h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow RianSweetDoris<br/>6<br/>54<br/>208<br/>[40K](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris/status/2060399898798018696/analytics)<br/>Charles Darwin. William Shakespeare. Emily Dickinson. Thomas Edison.<br/>What did all of these people have in common?<br/>They all had a place. A specific location in time and space from which they changed the world. And the nature of that place affected the degree of change they were able to make.<br/>From Edison's laboratory in Menlo Park to Darwin's family Down House in Kent, these creators had a workspace in which they lost themselves in a state of flow.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJfr73oXQAESnQ8?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris/article/2060399898798018696/media/2060374768323346433)<br/>Flow can be amplified or blocked by where we work. The trouble is, very few knowledge workers have intentionally designed their workspace for it.<br/>They tend to fall into one of three camps. Which of these sounds like you?<br/># <br/>I. THE THREE ARCHETYPES<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJf2PfwXQAMBKaN?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris/article/2060399898798018696/media/2060386100628111363)<br/>## <br/>1\. The Overwhelmed Office Worker<br/>You work in an open office plan plagued by constant disruptions. Between tap-on-the-shoulder meetings and chatter, you can't focus for more than a few minutes at a time. You dream of a private office, but feel powerless to change your environment.<br/>## <br/>2\. The Uninspired Home Worker<br/>Yesterday's dirty mugs still clutter your makeshift desk. Maybe you work on the couch, hunched over, so the ergonomics are terrible. The cluttered space is associated more with distraction than meaningful work. You know you could have a lovely office, but lack the motivation to set it up.<br/>## <br/>3\. The Entrepreneurial Athlete<br/>The Entrepreneurial Athlete engineers their workspace for flawless focus and flow. It's treated as a sacred and cherished space, so all sources of distraction are banned. Everything is set up for complete immersion and accelerated progress.<br/>The Entrepreneurial Athlete understands their environment must facilitate focus before focus can facilitate greatness.<br/>![](https://pbs.twimg.com/tweet_video_thumb/HJf4RUzXwAIZq-W.jpg)<br/>GIF<br/>Whichever camp you fall into, it's only after you optimize your workspace that you realize the magnitude of difference it can make to your focus, productivity, and access to flow.<br/>Your workspace is one of several specific blockers that quietly prevent access to flow state, the state of consciousness where you feel your best and perform your best. <br/>The workspace is where I start with almost everyone, because it's the lever they're most certain doesn't matter, and it's usually the one holding them back the most.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJf6AX5WkAsr8cX?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris/article/2060399898798018696/media/2060390238866804747)<br/># <br/>II. THE FLOW DOJO<br/>I once rented an oppressively bleak office space. 50th floor of a drab Mexico City high-rise. <br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJf5EWuXQAQxmAL?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2060357382052454403/RianSweetDoris/article/206</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 351 · 🔁 58 · 💬 11 · 🔖 868</p><p><a href="https://x.com/RianSweetDoris/status/2060399898798018696">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
