## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJiSYLrWkAUJiw4?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898/ashwingop/article/2060562561456611586/media/2060557773671141381) The Infinite White Monkey Theorem...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Ashwin Gopinath</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/ashwingop">@ashwingop</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898">http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898">http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJiSYLrWkAUJiw4?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898/ashwingop/article/2060562561456611586/media/2060557773671141381)<br/>The Infinite White Monkey Theorem <br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1591178826813317121/CXCC9Co8_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898/ashwingop)<br/>[Ashwin Gopinath![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1982955195307884544/Hn-rvttT_bigger.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898/ashwingop)<br/>[@ashwingop](http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898/ashwingop)<br/>·<br/>4h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow ashwingop<br/>1<br/>6<br/>[836](http://x.com/i/article/2060551779188645898/ashwingop/status/2060562561456611586/analytics)<br/>The <br/>[Infinite Monkey Theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infinite%5Fmonkey%5Ftheorem)<br/> used to be charming. Put a monkey in front of a typewriter, give it infinite time, and eventually it produces Shakespeare. Elegant and profoundly useless: one animal, one machine, one miracle achieved through statistical violence. The monkey did not need to understand Hamlet, monarchy, ghosts, or why Shakespeare’s characters need group therapy. It just had to keep hitting keys until infinity did the heavy lifting. That was the old world. The monkey was alone. The worst thing it could do was produce nonsense.<br/>It had no compiler, no autocomplete, no GitHub account, and definitely no AI agent whispering that what it meant was a full-stack SaaS platform with Supabase auth and tasteful gradients.<br/>The modern monkey has Cursor, Lovable or whichever AI coding tool is currently being described on X as “insane” by a man whose profile picture was generated inside a protein shake for lookmaxxing. This monkey is hunched over a MacBook that is now legally a biohazard, sticky banana fingers slapping Command-K like a lab rat discovering venture capital. It thinks Postgres is a skin condition and authentication means screeching until the rectangle apologizes.<br/>Still, it builds, because the new machine does not require deep understanding. The monkey can begin with:<br/>“make app”<br/>Then:<br/>“no make better”<br/>Then:<br/>“make it look like Linear but with bananas”<br/>Then:<br/>“why error you stupid robot”<br/>Followed by:<br/>“if this breaks again I will eat the .env file”<br/>Then, after the model fixes one import path:<br/>“ok thank you love u”<br/>By dawn, something exists. But, not Shakespeare, something way more dangerous: a startup!<br/>You have seen these things on X. Someone posts at 2:14 AM: “Built this over the weekend with no code. Already at $4k MRR.” The screenshot shows gradients, pricing, and a dashboard that says “Revenue” despite there being no customers and no evidence the founder has interacted with another mammal since Thursday. The app is always familiar: AI CRM for dentists, founder voice notes, or a knowledge base for people with too many knowledge bases. The frontend looks expensive with dithering and dark mode. The backend has worms and somewhere in the repo there is NEXT\_PUBLIC\_STRIPE\_SECRET\_KEY\_DONT\_STEAL\_PLEASE\_IM\_BEGGING\_YOU, four auth systems, and a hardcoded childhood-dog password. The monkey then posts: “Shipping fast. DMs open.” and the other monkeys upvote solemnly.<br/>I thought this was the Infinite Vibe Monkey Theorem. The monkey will later insist the correct name is worse.<br/>The old theorem was randomness plus infinity producing meaning. The new one is vibes plus models producing software, or at least enough rounded corners to survive a demo call. A random monkey makes garbage, licks something, and goes to sleep. This monkey is worse because it is productive (Note: productive ≠ useful). First it builds a CRUD app, then it builds another CRUD app to manage the first CRUD app and by sunrise, it has a customer portal, Stripe checkout, manifesto, and no idea what problem it is solvin</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 47 · 🔁 6 · 💬 5 · 🔖 71</p><p><a href="https://x.com/ashwingop/status/2060562561456611586">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
