## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJgFznya4AAUddx?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/aparnadhinak/article/2060406977357070522/media/2060403213933928448) Hermes Harness Architecture...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Aparna Dhinakaran</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/aparnadhinak">@aparnadhinak</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344">http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344">http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJgFznya4AAUddx?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/aparnadhinak/article/2060406977357070522/media/2060403213933928448)<br/>Hermes Harness Architecture<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1258117513872728064/c5A0LbF5_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/aparnadhinak)<br/>[Aparna Dhinakaran![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2051433378692640768/Ph-ysq0G_bigger.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/aparnadhinak)<br/>[@aparnadhinak](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/aparnadhinak)<br/>·<br/>7h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow aparnadhinak<br/>4<br/>43<br/>253<br/>[18K](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/aparnadhinak/status/2060406977357070522/analytics)<br/>Hermes (from <br/>[@NousResearch](https://x.com/@NousResearch)<br/>) is one of the best open-source harnesses in the ecosystem right now. We wanted to look at the implementation directly and map what we found to the framework we use for analyzing harnesses. <br/>In our earlier piece, "<br/>[What is an Agent Harness,](https://x.com/aparnadhinak/status/2046980769747533830)<br/>" we used a nine-part model:<br/>* outer iteration loop<br/>* context management and compression<br/>* skills and tools management<br/>* subagent management<br/>* built-in pre-packaged skills<br/>* session persistence and recovery<br/>* system prompt assembly with project context injection<br/>* lifecycle hooks<br/>* permission and safety layer<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJf_swpbUAMYq0L?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/aparnadhinak/article/2060406977357070522/media/2060396498983276547)<br/>Hermes implements all nine. The interesting details are in how it implements them, and in the extra systems that sit outside this frame.<br/>## <br/>The outer iteration loop<br/>The core loop is familiar: model call, tool dispatch, tool result append, repeat until final response or interrupt.<br/>Where Hermes is stronger than most open harnesses is provider abstraction. The same runtime can drive chat-completions style APIs, Anthropic Messages, Codex Responses, an out-of-process Codex app-server path, and Bedrock. Tool-call formats and provider quirks are normalized by transport adapters. At the loop level, the model surface looks consistent. For those of us, who really like having an open harness provider who supports all models, this open approach is much better than Claude Code.<br/>## <br/>Context management and compression<br/>Hermes has a full compression path, not a simplistic context trim.<br/>Older turns are summarized by an auxiliary model. Head and tail segments are protected by token budget. Tool outputs that are older than a configurable threshold are pruned before summarization. The summary budget scales with compressed content at roughly twenty percent, with a two-thousand-token floor and a twelve-thousand-token ceiling. In practice this avoids spending too much context on tiny compressions while still giving large compressions enough room to be useful.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJf0aooa8AAyhft?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2060381072148537344/aparnadhinak/article/2060406977357070522/media/2060384092966023168)<br/>Compression is also a session lifecycle event. On compression, Hermes closes the current SQLite session row, creates a child session seeded by the summary, rotates the session ID, and records parent-child lineage. Plugin context engines and memory providers are notified that a boundary moved. If a long conversation compresses multiple times, you get a lineage chain instead of one repeatedly rewritten transcript. This is pretty unique relative to other harness architectures we've reviewed.<br/>## <br/>Skills and tools management<br/>Tool registration and tool exposure are separate concerns in Hermes.<br/>Tools register into a central registry at import time. A separate toolset l</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 898 · 🔁 125 · 💬 13 · 🔖 1,973</p><p><a href="https://x.com/aparnadhinak/status/2060406977357070522">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
