## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJgjfuya8AAKe_G?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233/posthog/article/2060439373074878615/media/2060435857564430336) Stop adding AI between you and your customers [![Square profile...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>PostHog</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/posthog">@posthog</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233">http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233">http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJgjfuya8AAKe_G?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233/posthog/article/2060439373074878615/media/2060435857564430336)<br/>Stop adding AI between you and your customers<br/>[![Square profile picture](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2051741479433822210/YboW6yWw_normal.png)](http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233/posthog)<br/>[PostHog](http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233/posthog)<br/>[@posthog](http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233/posthog)<br/>·<br/>5h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow posthog<br/>2<br/>25<br/>[2K](http://x.com/i/article/2060433567768031233/posthog/status/2060439373074878615/analytics)<br/>You've heard it a thousand times, and for a while it's where the whole industry was heading. It was backwards then, and it gets more backwards by the day: "Support is expensive – put a chatbot in front of it."<br/>Here's the bet I'd make instead: as AI eats the easy parts of support, support should be more human and more technical than ever – an engineer, not a friendly face on top of the machine. The more your customers live inside AI, the more they'll want a technical human when something breaks.<br/># <br/>The evolution of support, in three eras<br/>## <br/>Then:<br/>Customers do all their work as humans. They write code, build charts, dig through data, make decisions. When something breaks, they write to a person, and a person writes back. Support is human-to-human because everything is human-to-human.<br/>## <br/>Now:<br/>AI shows up in customer workflows, but humans still do most of the work. Support, meanwhile, sits where it always has: treated as a cost to keep down. AI gives companies a new lever to pull: stick a chatbot in front of the queue, deflect as many tickets as you can, and hire fewer people. AI becomes a wall between the customer and the team.<br/>## <br/>Next:<br/>Where we're heading. AI does more and more of the customer's actual work. It analyzes the data. It builds the chart. It writes the code. It <br/>[tells them what to focus on next](https://posthog.com/blog/self-driving-product)<br/>. The customer's job becomes reviewing AI output, redirecting it, and deciding what to ship.<br/>The industry expectation is that support follows the same curve: more AI work means more AI support.<br/>That's backwards. If your customer spends all day talking to AI, the last thing they want when something goes wrong is to talk to another one. They want a person who can actually understand the problem, fix it, and makes them feel looked after rather than processed.<br/>The deeper customers go into AI, the more problems call for a human on the other end – and a more technical one than support has ever needed.<br/># <br/>Why "AI as blocker" was always a bad idea<br/>As our support team lead, I've been asked plenty of times what I think of putting AI in front of our support queue. I've always said no.<br/>Not because AI isn't useful, but because there's a difference between AI as a tool and AI as a gate.<br/>A gate is something you have to get past, and it frustrates customers. Support chatbots only handle the simple stuff – the questions already answered in the docs – so anyone with a harder problem learns to type "agent" or "human" three times to reach actual help. <br/>No one is trying to be deliberately frustrating, it's just what happens when a harder problem runs into a wall built for easy ones.<br/>Most companies don't realize the frustration they're creating. They look at the numbers, see fewer tickets coming in, and read it as a good sign: customers must be doing fine. Companies call it "deflection." Customers call it being ignored.<br/>The result is the same either way: some people get frustrated and give up on raising a ticket at all. They quietly churn, and you've lost them without ever getting the chance to speak.<br/>Why do companies let it get that far? Because of a framing that's been around</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 49 · 🔁 1 · 🔖 73</p><p><a href="https://x.com/posthog/status/2060439373074878615">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
