## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJetLEpWgAMG81L?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576/Zephyr%5Fhg/article/2060305898182971806/media/2060305760282640387) When AI Stops Saving You Time And Starts Slowing You Down (You Won't Notice The Day It Happens)....</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Zephyr</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/Zephyr_hg">@Zephyr_hg</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576">http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576">http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJetLEpWgAMG81L?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576/Zephyr%5Fhg/article/2060305898182971806/media/2060305760282640387)<br/>When AI Stops Saving You Time And Starts Slowing You Down (You Won't Notice The Day It Happens).<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1741076613939277824/vs_v9C1j_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576/Zephyr%5Fhg)<br/>[Zephyr](http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576/Zephyr%5Fhg)<br/>[@Zephyr\_hg](http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576/Zephyr%5Fhg)<br/>·<br/>13h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow Zephyr\_hg<br/>1<br/>1<br/>25<br/>[2.7K](http://x.com/i/article/2060304215818264576/Zephyr%5Fhg/status/2060305898182971806/analytics)<br/>There's a point in heavy AI use where the time-saving curve flattens, then bends down.<br/>You don't notice the day it happens. You notice the lag a few weeks later, when work that used to take an afternoon now eats the whole day. The deliverables look the same. The hours don't.<br/>That's the threshold. It's real. Most people cross it inside 9-12 months of heavy AI use and never name it.<br/>Here's how it happens.<br/>## <br/>The 5 Mechanisms<br/>1\. Cognitive offloading. Your brain stops doing the prep work. When you sit down to write something, your first instinct is "let me ask Claude" instead of thinking it through. The 30 seconds Claude saves costs you the 5 minutes of mental compounding you'd have done before reaching the keyboard.<br/>2\. Decision paralysis. You generate 10 drafts because it's cheap. Now you have to pick the right one. The decision cost replaces the writing cost. Net effect: slower.<br/>3\. Re-prompting loops. You ask for X. You get Y. You iterate. By prompt 4 you've spent more time than if you'd just done it yourself. The loops feel productive because each prompt feels like progress.<br/>4\. Verification overhead. Every Claude output needs review. The review time often takes 60-80% as long as doing the work yourself would have.<br/>5\. Taste atrophy. Without the friction of doing things yourself, your standards drift. You start accepting work that's "fine" because you forgot what "good" felt like coming out of your own hands.<br/>## <br/>The Signals You've Crossed<br/>You won't notice the day it happens. But you'll notice these signals if you look for them.<br/>You can't remember the last time you wrote a full paragraph without Claude.<br/>You ask Claude things you'd have Googled 2 years ago.<br/>You feel slower at solo tasks than you did 6 months ago.<br/>You don't actually know what's in your own deliverables anymore.<br/>You feel a vague exhaustion at the end of "AI-assisted" days that you didn't feel on equivalent non-AI days a year ago.<br/>Three or more of these is the threshold. Most heavy users cross it in 2026.<br/>## <br/>Why The Math Inverts<br/>In month 1, Claude saves you 40% of the time on a typical task.<br/>In month 12, the calculation looks different. The same task now includes prompt framing, context loading, output review, re-prompts, decisions between variants, and the slow taste drift you can't see.<br/>Add it up honestly and most people are net-zero by month 12 on solo work. On collaborative work, they're often net-negative.<br/>The math hasn't changed. Your relationship to the work changed.<br/>## <br/>The 3 Corrections That Work<br/>1\. The "What would I do without Claude?" test. Before every task, ask the question. If the honest answer is "I'd just do it in 10 minutes," do it. The 10 minutes is the cheapest version.<br/>2\. The 3-prompt rule. If you've iterated past 3 prompts and the output still isn't right, the prompt isn't the problem. The task is. Close Claude. Finish it yourself.<br/>3\. The output-not-input rule. Use Claude for output: deliverables, drafts, transformations. Avoid using Claude for input: thinking, deciding, framing. The first compounds your work. The second compounds dependence.<br/>## <br/>What</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 29 · 🔁 2 · 💬 2 · 🔖 66</p><p><a href="https://x.com/Zephyr_hg/status/2060305898182971806">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
